When nothing listens for an untrusted event, Ladybird skips building
its path and running its dispatch phases, sets its target to the target
itself, and still runs the steps that end a dispatch. It does that only
outside a shadow tree, for an event with no related or touch targets.
WebKit takes a similar shortcut in EventDispatcher::dispatchEvent(): If
the document has no listeners for the type, nothing has a default event
handler, and neither the node nor the relatedTarget is in a shadow tree,
it sets the target and returns before resetAfterDispatch(). So in WebKit
26.5, cancelBubble stays true after such a dispatch, and a relatedTarget
in a shadow tree isn’t retargeted. Blink’s EventDispatcher::Dispatch()
always builds the event path, and Gecko’s EventDispatcher::Dispatch()
always builds the event target chain. In 12 variants (detached nodes,
text nodes, fragments, shadow trees with and without composed, slotted
nodes, related targets in and out of shadow trees, re-dispatch, flags
set before dispatch, the document, non-bubbling events), Ladybird now
gives the same results as Chrome 157.0.8103.78 and Firefox 151.0.
